The N-I-O path — modern filesystem paths.

The second half of the module turns to files — the everyday place where input-output and exceptions meet. Modern Java has two file APIs, and the first thing to learn is which one to use. The old java-io-file, from nineteen ninety-six, is still around, but effectively retired. Everything new goes through java-nio-file — the N-I-O-point-two API, introduced in Java seven — built around two types: path, a location, and files, operations on it. This section is about path; the next is about files.

So what is a path — and what isn't it? A path is an abstract location in a filesystem — a sequence of name elements, like home, then ada, then notes-dot-txt. And crucially, it is just the address. Creating a path touches no disk, and the file it names need not exist. It's a value you manipulate — build it, join it, resolve it — before you ever read or write. You make one with path-dot-of. Path-dot-of, of the string slash-home-slash-ada-slash-notes-dot-txt, is the modern factory, from Java eleven. And path-dot-of, of the segments "docs" and "report-dot-p-d-f", joins them with the platform's separator — so you write portable code, not hard-coded slashes.

Now, building paths. The two core operations combine and compare paths, and they're inverses of each other. Given a base of slash-home-slash-ada, base-dot-resolve of "notes-dot-txt" gives you slash-home-slash-ada-slash-notes-dot-txt — it joins a child. And base-dot-relativize of that file gives you back just "notes-dot-txt" — the route from the base to the file. So resolve appends — that's the right way to build a child path, instead of gluing strings with a slash. And relativize computes the relative route between two paths. Prefer these to string concatenation: they respect the separator, and handle the edge cases — absolute versus relative segments — correctly.

Path also has a vocabulary for inspecting and normalizing, and these are all pure, string-level operations, still touching no disk. Get-file-name gives the last element, notes-dot-txt. Get-parent gives everything but the last, slash-home-slash-ada. Is-absolute tells you whether it's rooted or relative. Normalize collapses a-slash-dot-slash-b-slash-dot-dot-slash-c down to a-slash-c. And to-absolute-path resolves a relative path against the working directory. Normalize earns special mention: it collapses the single-dot and double-dot segments, which matters for security — it defuses a dot-dot-slash traversal attack, like dot-dot-slash-dot-dot-slash-etc-slash-passwd — and it matters for comparing two paths that point to the same place by different routes.

Finally, why path over the old file? The switch isn't cosmetic — java-nio-file fixes real deficiencies of java-io-file. First, honest errors. Old file methods returned false on failure — so if file-dot-delete returned false, did it fail, or was the file already gone? You couldn't tell. The N-I-O files operations throw a descriptive input-output-exception instead — that's the section-one lesson, applied to the filesystem. Second, it's full-featured: symbolic links, file attributes and permissions, atomic moves, directory-change watching — capabilities that file never had. And third, a clean separation: path is the location, files is the operations, whereas the old file muddled both into one class.

So here's the mental model to carry into the next sections. A path is an address you build, and reason about, with pure, disk-free operations. And files is where that address finally meets the disk — and where the input-output exceptions from the first half of this module come home.
